course: dictionary
lesson: 9083
title: Friendly teasing words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A word can be a joke between friends. «Come on, slowcoach!» someone says to a friend who is lagging behind on the way to school. The words around it are the clues: come on tells you to hurry, so slowcoach means a person who is too slow.
explain: Listen to how the speaker sounds. The sentence is teasing, not cruel, because the speaker wants you to catch up with them. Context tells you the tone as well as the meaning.
explain: The phrase «don't be such a slowcoach» shows that the word is informal. People use it in friendly chat, not in a formal letter or a school report.
fact: Slowcoach is a noun, so you can have a slowcoach or several slowcoaches.
fact: «Slowcoach» means a person who does things too slowly, said in a teasing, friendly way.
fact: Slowcoach is used mostly in the UK, Ireland and Australia. In the US, people say slowpoke for the same idea.
fact: Phrases you may hear include «come on, slowcoach» and «don't be such a slowcoach».
fact: In another language, «tormoz», which literally means brake, is used in a similar way: it means someone slow to get a joke, and it is mildly mean but fine between pals.
exercise: «Come on, slowcoach!» What does slowcoach mean? | A coach who drives a bus | *A person who does things too slowly | A person who sleeps in late on holidays | A fast runner who wins every race
exercise: Grandad hears a friend say «Don't be such a slowcoach.» How does the speaker probably feel? | Scared of the dark | Very angry and wanting to fight | Sad and close to tears | *Teasing, but in a friendly way
exercise: Which word means the same as slowcoach in the US? | coachman | speedster | *slowpoke | racer
test: Ella's friend says «You're such a slowcoach!» and laughs. Is the joke mean or friendly? | A brand of bicycle | A warning about the weather | Cruel and hurtful | *Friendly, with a laugh
test: Which phrase tells you a friend wants you to hurry up? | *Come on, slowcoach | Take your time, please | Stop right there | Well done, champion
exam: Which is the best meaning of slowcoach in «Come on, slowcoach, we'll be late!»? | A very clever person | A famous runner | *Someone too slow, said in a teasing way | A kind of train
